<description>
Move magenta particles along corridors formed by parallel colored bars.

Input structure:
- bg=7. Particles=6 in col 0. Colored bars on even rows form corridors on odd rows.

Rule:
- Particle goes straight through bg cells.
- When blocked by a colored cell, turns 90° into the perpendicular direction
  that has a bg cell flanked by same-color bars. Stops at grid edge or full blockage.

Key insight: Same-color parallel bars define the only valid corridors, so each particle follows a deterministic local trace.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "magenta particle pixel in leftmost column at valid corridor entrances",
  "path": "corridor, i.e. empty space between two parallel bars of same color, with inertia principle",
  "trajectory": "none",
  "dst": "grid edge",
  "notes": "inertia principle: particle maintains direction until forced to change by obstacle or available turn"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 17-23 rows (odd) x 14-21 cols; bg=7 (orange), particle=6 (magenta)
  col_0: all particle (6); col_1: all bg (7)
  colors: exactly 6 distinct (not 6/7); 3-6 corridor colors, 0-3 decorator colors

  layout:
    row: striped — even rows = bar rows, odd rows = channel or bg
    col: vertical corridors at even columns; vertical bars at adjacent odd columns
    even_row_fill: 100% — every even row has at least one bar (corridor or decorator)
    particles_move_from: odd rows only

  corridor:
    definition: same-color bar pair flanking a 1-cell channel; bars start at col 2-3
    turns: 0-3 per corridor via L-shaped same-color bar extensions
    L-turn_seal: at the turn, same_bar and opp_bar both extend to the outer wall column; opp_bar is continuous (no gap at v_channel) — seals the L so particles can only enter from the turn direction (solver asserts: corridor ≥ 2 rows at turn target AND opp_bar has color at v_channel)
    multi-turn: S/U-shaped corridors use two channel rows of the SAME color connected by vertical bars
    gap_traversal: particles go straight through bg (including crossroad gaps) — no special inertia logic needed

  crossings:
    definition: where a vertical corridor crosses a horizontal corridor (real or fake)
    behavior: pass-through OR complete block — never enter from one bar and get trapped by the other
    pass-through: both bars of the crossed corridor gap at the v_channel column; particle crosses with inertia
    block: neither bar gaps; particle is stopped outside the crossed corridor

  decorator:
    purpose: distractor — forms fake corridors that are structurally indistinguishable from real corridors except for bar color mismatch
    placement: single bars on bar rows not used by corridor pairs; same gap logic as corridor bars at crossings
    fake_corridor: BG cell flanked by two different-color bars; must be as passable as real corridors (fake/real path ratio 0.5-2.0)

  particles: 3-6 move from (row, 0) rightward; path length 14-46 cells
  movement:
    rule: go straight through bg cells; when blocked by a colored cell, turn 90°
    turn: check both perpendicular directions for a bg cell flanked by same-color bars; exactly one should be valid (solver asserts no ambiguous tie-break)
    stop: grid edge or no valid turn when blocked

  quantitative_constraints (ALL REQUIRED — generator MUST satisfy these, not optional):
    corridor_pixels: 26-39% of grid
    decorator_pixels: 0-13% of grid
    real_corridor_path: 11-18% of grid (BG cells between same-color bar pairs)
    fake_corridor_path: 9-23% of grid (BG cells between different-color bar pairs)
    turn_distribution: {0: 25-40%, 1: 40-55%, 2+: 10-25%}
    turn>=1_edge_rate: 60-90%
    turn>=2_edge_rate: 20-50%

generation_strategy:
  approach: path-first — design trajectories first, then build walls around them
  steps:
    1. Grid H×W (H odd). Striped layout: even rows = bar, odd rows = channel/bg. Vertical structure at even columns.
    2. Plan real trajectories (3-6): each is a sequence of H/V segments on odd-row channels. Assign same-color bar pairs around each. For multi-turn paths, connect two channels of the same color with vertical bars.
    3. Plan fake trajectories on remaining odd rows: same structural rules (segments, turns, crossings) but flanked by different-color bars. Fake trajectories should be as passable as real ones — apply the same crossing gap logic.
    4. Build bars around all trajectories (real + fake). The only difference between real and fake is whether the two flanking bars share a color.
    5. Col 0 = particle, col 1 = bg. Validate with solver and quantitative constraints.
```
</generation_spec>
